          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is it ever correct to submit a code root without its decimal characters?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** Usually no. A category root such as E11 is not a complete code. The one exception in the corpus is I10, which is complete at three characters.\n\n- **E11 (type 2 diabetes):** \"E11 is never used at three characters.\" The fourth and later characters name the complication, and a code ending in 9 (E11.9) covers diabetes documented without complications. Submitting bare E11 is not an acceptable choice. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.)\n- **J45 (asthma):** \"The category is never used at three characters.\" The fourth and later characters record severity and the state of the disease. (Source: *Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes*.)\n- **I10 (essential hypertension):** This is \"unusual in being a complete code at three characters.\" Adding characters to I10 is not possible, so a request for more specificity cannot be met with this code. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*.)\n\nSo the rule is code-specific. Whether a three-character code is complete depends on that code's own entry, not on a general convention. The corpus does not set out a full set of submission or billing rules, so I cannot confirm from it how payers handle every case. For claims, check the current official code file and the payer's guidance, or ask a certified medical coder. Code validity also changes between years, so the code set in use on the date of service matters.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
